C = [
("h1", "8. The Record and the Powers"),
("p", "Claim: Colossians 2:13–15 contains the densest cluster of figures for the Cross in Paul’s letters, and the figures are three: a record blotted out, an act of nailing, and a triumph over principalities and powers. The passage says what each does in a single run of verbs. It does not identify the record in detail, and it does not identify the principalities and powers. The volume reports it as written and says where it stops."),
("sub", "8.1 The sentence"),
("p", "“And you, being dead in your sins and the uncircumcision of your flesh, hath he quickened together with him, having forgiven you all trespasses; Blotting out the handwriting of ordinances that was against us, which was contrary to us, and took it out of the way, nailing it to his cross; And having spoiled principalities and powers, he made a shew of them openly, triumphing over them in it” (Colossians 2:13–15). One sentence in the English; five participles hang from the verb “hath he quickened.” They are forgiving, blotting out, taking away, nailing, and spoiling. The sentence is addressed to “you,” who were dead in sins and the uncircumcision of the flesh."),
("ctbl", "What Colossians 2:13–15 says and what it leaves unsaid.", [
    ("The clause", "What it says", "What it leaves unsaid"),
    ("“you, being dead in your sins and the uncircumcision of your flesh, hath he quickened together with him”", "The readers were dead and have been made alive together with Christ", "The mechanism of quickening; the sentence gives the fact"),
    ("“having forgiven you all trespasses”", "All trespasses forgiven", "Whether forgiveness precedes or accompanies the quickening; the participle does not say"),
    ("“Blotting out the handwriting of ordinances that was against us”", "A handwriting of ordinances was against “us”, and was blotted out", "What the handwriting is; whether it is the law, a record of debt, or both; the clause says only “of ordinances”"),
    ("“and took it out of the way, nailing it to his cross”", "It was taken out of the way, and nailed to his cross", "How the nailing deals with it; the figure is stated, not explained"),
    ("“having spoiled principalities and powers, he made a shew of them openly, triumphing over them in it”", "Principalities and powers were spoiled, shown openly, and triumphed over", "Who they are; whether they are angels, evil or good, or human rulers; the sentence names them only by the two words"),
  ], [0.33, 0.33, 0.34]),
("sub", "8.2 The handwriting"),
("p", "The King James Version has “the handwriting of ordinances that was against us.” The word translated handwriting is a written document, and the phrase is joined to “ordinances.” The verse says that it was against “us” and “contrary to us.” It says three things were done with it: it was blotted out, it was taken out of the way, and it was nailed to the cross. The verse does not say that it was the law as a whole. It says that it was “of ordinances.” Thomas Constable reads it as what “probably refers to God’s record of our sins,” and he says it applies to Jews who violated the Law of Moses and to Gentiles who violated the law written on the heart. The volume takes the figure in his sense as a record, and stops where the verse does."),
("p", "The ruling that guides the volume is this: the record is said in the passage to have been taken out of the way for the readers, and the volume uses the wording that fits the letter to the Ephesians, where the middle wall of partition is broken down and the enmity abolished “in his flesh” for the Body. The volume does not say that the record is gone for any other company. It does not say that Israel’s law has been abolished as Israel’s. The jurisdiction of the law, whom it was given to and where its reach ended, is the subject of the study on the law’s jurisdiction, and this volume does not repeat it."),
("sub", "8.3 The principalities and powers"),
("p", "“And having spoiled principalities and powers, he made a shew of them openly, triumphing over them in it” (Colossians 2:15). The verse names two words, “principalities” and “powers.” The sentence says that Christ spoiled them, made a show of them openly, and triumphed over them “in it.” The pronoun can be read as the cross or as himself, and the volume leaves it open. Constable reads the powers as what “probably refers to Christ’s defeat of Satan and his evil demonic assistants by His death and resurrection.”"),
("p", "The volume does not identify the powers. It is a plain fact of the text that the verse does not say whether they are good or evil, angelic or human, or whether they hold a place in the heavens or on the earth. The sentence gives no further detail. The volume does not draw a map of the unseen world from the verse. It reports that the Cross is here said to have spoiled them, shown them openly, and triumphed over them."),
("sub", "8.4 Three figures, three results"),
("tbl", "The three figures of Colossians 2:14–15.", [
    ("Verse", "KJV text", "What to observe"),
    ("Col 2:14", "“Blotting out the handwriting of ordinances that was against us”", "The record is removed; it stood against us"),
    ("Col 2:14", "“took it out of the way, nailing it to his cross”", "The removal is located at the cross"),
    ("Col 2:15", "“having spoiled principalities and powers”", "The powers are spoiled"),
    ("Col 2:15", "“he made a shew of them openly, triumphing over them in it”", "A public triumph"),
  ], [0.14, 0.50, 0.36]),
("sub", "8.5 What follows in the chapter"),
("p", "The next verse is the conclusion Paul draws: “Let no man therefore judge you in meat, or in drink, or in respect of an holyday, or of the new moon, or of the sabbath days: Which are a shadow of things to come; but the body is of Christ” (Colossians 2:16, 17). The word “therefore” ties the removal of the record to the instruction not to be judged by such things. The sabbath days are the subject of another study in this series, and the volume does not take them up here. The point for this chapter is that the passage’s result is practical: because the record has been taken out of the way, no man is to judge the readers by the ordinances. The Cross in Colossians 2 answers a pressure to submit to ordinances, and the answer is that the record is gone and the powers are spoiled."),
]
